A document with nested structure, directly extending plain JavaScript object syntax from Module 4:

{
  _id: ObjectId('...'),
  name: 'Maria Lopez',
  email: 'maria@example.com',
  address: {
    street: '123 Oak St',
    city: 'Austin',
    zip: '78701'
  },
  tags: ['vip', 'newsletter-subscriber'],
  orders: [
    { orderId: 'ord_1', total: 49.99, date: '2026-01-15' },
    { orderId: 'ord_2', total: 120.00, date: '2026-02-03' }
  ]
}

Accessing nested data once retrieved in your Node.js application code works exactly like accessing any nested JavaScript object/array, since (as lesson 3 emphasized) a MongoDB document essentially IS a JavaScript object once loaded: user.address.city, user.orders[0].total, user.tags.includes('vip') — all standard JavaScript, no new syntax required for working with already-retrieved data, only for querying it directly inside MongoDB itself (covered in lesson 7).

