sections.content:
Embedding (full data nested directly inside the parent, as in lesson 4):
{
  _id: ObjectId('post1'),
  title: 'My First Post',
  comments: [
    { author: 'Sam', text: 'Nice post!' },
    { author: 'Lee', text: 'Thanks for sharing' }
  ]
}

Referencing (related data lives in its own collection, parent holds only an _id reference):
// posts collection:
{ _id: ObjectId('post1'), title: 'My First Post' }

// comments collection, each referencing its post:
{ _id: ObjectId('c1'), postId: ObjectId('post1'), author: 'Sam', text: 'Nice post!' }
{ _id: ObjectId('c2'), postId: ObjectId('post1'), author: 'Lee', text: 'Thanks for sharing' }

With referencing, retrieving a post's comments requires a separate query against the comments collection, filtering by postId — conceptually identical to the products/reviews relationship you've already built in Module 7, lesson 4, just now backed by real MongoDB collections instead of in-memory arrays.

sections.code:
import { ObjectId } from 'mongodb';

// Creating a comment that references its parent post
app.post('/posts/:postId/comments', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    if (!req.body.text) {
      return res.status(400).json({ success: false, error: 'text is required' });
    }

    const newComment = {
      postId: new ObjectId(req.params.postId), // the reference itself
      author: req.body.author,
      text: req.body.text
    };

    const result = await commentsCollection.insertOne(newComment);
    res.status(201).json({ success: true, data: { _id: result.insertedId, ...newComment } });
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);
  }
});

// Retrieving a post AND its comments, requiring two separate queries
app.get('/posts/:postId/full', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const postId = new ObjectId(req.params.postId);

    const post = await postsCollection.findOne({ _id: postId });
    if (!post) return res.status(404).json({ success: false, error: 'Post not found' });

    const comments = await commentsCollection.find({ postId: postId }).toArray();

    res.json({ success: true, data: { ...post, comments } });
  } catch (err) {
    next(err);
  }
});

