// STEP 1 - Core entities: User, Post, Comment, Tag

// STEP 2 - Fields per entity:
// User: name, email, bio
// Post: title, body, createdAt, authorId
// Comment: text, authorId, postId, createdAt
// Tag: name

// STEP 3 - Relationship decisions (applying lesson 6's factors):
// - Post -> Author: REFERENCE (authorId). Users are independently
//   significant; many posts reference the same user.
// - Post -> Comments: REFERENCE (postId on each comment). Comments
//   can grow unboundedly on a popular post, and may need independent
//   moderation/pagination - exactly the lesson 6 'growth' factor.
// - Post -> Tags: EMBED (an array of tag name strings directly on
//   the post). Tags are small, bounded, and always shown with the
//   post itself - no independent querying of an individual tag
//   document is genuinely needed for this application's use cases.

// STEP 4 - Likely index candidates:
// - comments.postId (queried constantly to assemble a post's comments)
// - posts.authorId (querying "all posts by this user" is a likely feature)
// - posts.createdAt (sorting posts by recency is a near-certain feature)

// STEP 5 - Sample documents, written out by hand before any code:
// users collection:
{ _id: ObjectId('u1'), name: 'Maya Chen', email: 'maya@example.com', bio: 'Writer and editor' }

// posts collection:
{
  _id: ObjectId('p1'),
  title: 'Getting Started With Data Modeling',
  body: '...',
  authorId: ObjectId('u1'),
  tags: ['databases', 'beginners'],
